    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Dell down 34% in November on AI selloff, two days before FQ3 results",
               "summary":  "Dell closed at US$278.02 on 2026-11-20, down 11.04% in a day and about 53% below its US$595.51 52-week high. November is on pace to be its worst month since the stock relisted in 2018. Dell itself put out no bad news. The selling came with an industry-wide AI-hardware rout (Nov 5, Nov 13, Nov 16, Nov 20) and worries about memory costs and orders pulled forward. The business still has a US$95B AI-server backlog and guides to US$74B of FY27 AI revenue. Fiscal Q3 results on Nov 24 will test whether that backlog turns into revenue and cash.",
               "pros":  [
                            "The AI-server backlog is a record US$95B after US$60.9B of orders in fiscal Q2.",
                            "Dell raised FY27 guidance by US$25B to US$192B, including US$74B from AI servers.",
                            "Fiscal Q3 is guided to US$49B revenue (+81% YoY) and US$6.50 adjusted EPS, reported Nov 24.",
                            "The stock trades near 12x forward earnings, per TIKR, about half of Vertiv\u0027s multiple.",
                            "RSI is 28.46, below the 30 oversold line, after a 34% fall in one month."
